\subsection{LP algebras of finite mutation type} \label{sec:finitemutation}
As the following example shows, there are LP algebras of finite mutation type which do not fall into the cluster setting.

Take the coefficient ring $S = \Z$, ambient field $\FF = \Q(y_1,y_2,y_3)$ and initial seed $$t = \{(y_1, y_2+y_3+1),(y_2, y_1^2+y_1y_3+y_3^2), (y_3, y_2+y_1+1)\}.$$ Then one obtains the exchange graph shown in Figure \ref{fig:lp1}.  The initial seed corresponds to the vertex shared by the three bricks labeled $y_1$, $y_2$, and $y_3$.

